le","details":"ok","fails":[],"hint":"Each Then names what a test reads, using at least one of these words: response, status, returned, row, record, call, request, sent, email, message, error, event, field, value, count, list, file, screen, page, shown, displayed. \"Sign-in succeeds\" fails; \"The response status is 200\" passes.","passed":true},{"blocking":false,"check":"L12 literals","details":"ok","fails":[],"passed":true}],"roundTrip":{"droppedSpans":[],"inventedCapabilities":[]}},"problemOf":{"Q-12":[1],"Q-13":[2],"Q-14":[3]},"problems":[{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-9","text":"AC-9.1 says a stock request filtered to an out-of-scope warehouse returns 403, but AC-1.2 says scoped collection calls return a list limited to the caller's scope and the supplied filters, which implies 200 with an empty or filtered list."},{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-9","text":"REQ-9 says callers are authorized by 'PostgreSQL account roles and scopes', but no role-to-operation permission table is given or anchored in the contract (AC-9.1 just says 'the role permitted for stock listing'), so the 403 behaviour of most operations is undefined."},{"kind":"critic","reqId":"REQ-2","text":"REQ-2 uses EnsureCreated for empty databases and reviewed migrations for existing ones, but EnsureCreated writes no migrations-history table, so a database it creates cannot later take a migration; the spec also never defines 'genuinely empty' or how startup detects it, even though Program.cs currently calls EnsureCreated every time."}],"round":2,"settled":[{"decision":"How should schema changes be applied to databases that already exist when the new persistent tables are introduced? → Apply a reviewed database migration during deployment, preserving existing data; use EnsureCreated only for genuinely empty databases.","how":"answered","kind":"critic","problem":"The spec adds persistent entities but keeps startup on `EnsureCreated` (C-5), which does nothing once tables exist, and it has no migration or schema-evolution plan for existing databases despite the 'migration' risk tag.","ref":"Q-6"},{"decision":"With demo seeding disabled, how should the first PostgreSQL account be made available so the API can authorize callers? → Have deployment provision the initial account outside the API; do not add an API bootstrap flow.","how":"answered","kind":"critic","problem":"With Seed:Demo off and an empty database, no account exists, yet REQ-9 authorizes every caller from a PostgreSQL account record; the spec has no way to bootstrap the first account, and AC-5.1 assumes 'an account row provisioned by deployment' with no anchor for that process.","ref":"Q-7"},{"decision":"Which staff identity provider and concrete authorization-URL and token-validation settings should the API use? → Identify the organization's existing staff provider and supply its authorization URL and validation settings; do not introduce a new provider.","how":"answered","kind":"critic","problem":"REQ-7 has no anchors and refers to 'the existing staff identity provider', but current-behaviour shows no identity-provider integration or configuration anywhere in the codebase.","ref":"Q-8"},{"decision":"How should the test and factory runs obtain the valid nonproduction identity-provider settings required for startup? → Provide valid nonproduction settings for the chosen provider through test and factory configuration.","how":"answered","kind":"critic","problem":"Making startup fail on missing IdP settings will break the existing HealthTests.ApiIsUp and the factory runs, which supply no such settings today; REQ-7/REQ-8 claim these checks 'use valid nonproduction settings' without anchors and quietly require changing test and factory configuration.","ref":"Q-9"},{"decision":"How much customer-authentication behavior beyond the locked contract should be specified for failed logins and password storage? → Keep behavior limited to the locked contract; use standard secure password hashing for stored passwords, and do not add new lockout or one-time-code policies.","how":"answered","kind":"critic","problem":"REQ-9 has no error paths for customer authentication: wrong password, unknown email, wrong, expired or reused one-time code, and locked accounts are all missing; it also doesn't say how passwords are stored, despite the auth and pii risk tags.","ref":"Q-10"},{"decision":"Which token claim should identify the PostgreSQL staff account, and what should happen if a valid token has no matching or has a disabled account? → Match the token's email claim to the account email, and deny access when no matching enabled account exists.","how":"answered","kind":"critic","problem":"REQ-9 doesn't say how a staff IdP token maps to a PostgreSQL account record (which claim is matched), or what happens when a token is valid but has no account record, or a disabled one.","ref":"Q-11"}],"spec":{"assumptions":["ASM-1: For this specification, “a few” sample rows means between two and five rows per empty contract-backed table."],"nfrs":[],"outOfScope":["Portal screens and web-app implementation (I-5, Q-1).","Product-story behavior that is not defined by the locked OpenAPI contract (Q-1)."],"requirements":[{"acceptance":[{"given":"Every operation and response case defined in `contracts/openapi.yaml`, including contract-valid and contract-invalid requests and the declared authentication and authorization cases.","id":"AC-1.1","level":"api","then":"The response status, content type, response fields, and error body conform to that operation's OpenAPI request and response definitions.","when":"A test client sends the corresponding HTTP requests to every declared operation."},{"given":"A caller authenticated as an account with assigned warehouse or organization scopes calls a collection or record operation using contract-defined filters.","id":"AC-1.2","level":"api","then":"The response status and returned list or record contain only data within the caller's assigned scope and reflect the supplied filters.","when":"The caller sends the HTTP request to that operation."},{"given":"The API is running with valid startup configuration.","id":"AC-1.3","level":"api","then":"The response status for each request is 200.","when":"A client requests `GET /`, `GET /openapi/v1.json`, and `GET /swagger`."}],"anchors":[{"lineEnd":12,"lineStart":1,"path":"contracts/openapi.yaml","quote":"openapi: 3.0.3\ninfo:\n  title: Meridian Cold Chain API\n  version: 1.0.0\n  description: \"Shared contract for the Meridian responsive web portal.\"\nservers:\n  - url: /api\npaths:\n  /auth/staff/authorization-url:\n    get:\n      operationId: getStaffAuthorizationUrl\n      summary: \"Get the configured staff identity-provider authorization URL.\""},{"lineEnd":31,"lineStart":28,"path":"App.Api/Program.cs","quote":"app.MapGet(\"/\", () => \"API is up\").ExcludeFromDescription();\n// The API's own document (/openapi/v1.json) and a page to read and try it (/swagger). Keep both: the factory's product page links to the page.\napp.MapOpenApi();\napp.UseSwaggerUI(o => o.SwaggerEndpoint(\"/openapi/v1.json\", \"API\"));"}],"ears":"The Meridian API shall implement only the operations and behavior defined in `contracts/openapi.yaml`, with the existing root health, OpenAPI-document, and Swagger UI routes retained.","id":"REQ-1","op":"MODIFIED","sources":["I-1","I-5","Q-1"],"stability":1},{"acceptance":[{"given":"A configured PostgreSQL database and a valid request to a contract operation that creates or changes persistent business data.","id":"AC-2.1","level":"api","then":"The response status is the operation's contract-declared success status, the returned record identifier and values reflect the requested change, and the persisted row values match that change.","when":"A client calls the operation and then reads the affected entity through the corresponding contract operation or directly from the database."},{"given":"A configured PostgreSQL database and a valid receipt input.","id":"AC-2.2","level":"ap